WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.570 --> 00:00:05.070
From Pepsi references to "Supernatural" reunions
to Billy Butcher's secret love for his dog,

00:00:05.070 --> 00:00:08.620
Terror, there is no end to the incredibly
detailed Easter eggs hidden all throughout

00:00:08.620 --> 00:00:13.710
Amazon Prime's hit show. But only true fans
notice these things from Season 3 of "The

00:00:13.710 --> 00:00:14.710
Boys."

00:00:14.710 --> 00:00:19.010
In Season 3, The Boys find themselves going
up against an all-new supe: Soldier Boy. Technically,

00:00:19.010 --> 00:00:23.270
Soldier Boy isn't new to the superhero game.
He's the first powered-up living weapon Vought

00:00:23.270 --> 00:00:26.830
created, and he's been a part of the company
for a long time. According to the Vought-approved

00:00:26.830 --> 00:00:31.070
version of his biography, his service in World
War II included storming the beaches on D-Day,

00:00:31.070 --> 00:00:34.140
and he continued working with Vought into
the Vietnam War.

00:00:34.140 --> 00:00:38.329
Like Homelander, Soldier Boy has incredible
strength and impenetrable skin. He also doesn't

00:00:38.329 --> 00:00:42.530
seem to age and has more than a handful of
personality quirks, like compulsive marijuana

00:00:42.530 --> 00:00:47.600
smoking or casually homicidal tendencies to
cite two examples. It makes him a profoundly

00:00:47.600 --> 00:00:48.960
unreliable hero.

00:00:48.960 --> 00:00:52.899
Soldier Boy reportedly died in the 1980s.
At the beginning of the season, Butcher is

00:00:52.899 --> 00:00:56.970
investigating how the seemingly unbeatable
Soldier Boy was finally defeated. Maeve helps

00:00:56.970 --> 00:01:01.010
Butcher's investigation as her own form of
rebellion against Vought, and the intel that

00:01:01.010 --> 00:01:04.379
she gives him in the first episode of the
season also includes a hint at Soldier Boy's

00:01:04.379 --> 00:01:08.470
real personality. Maeve gives Butcher a file
on Soldier Boy which prominently features

00:01:08.470 --> 00:01:10.750
the name Raymond L. S. Patriarca.

00:01:10.750 --> 00:01:14.340
It's not clear whether Soldier Boy is this
universe's version of the real-world Rhode

00:01:14.340 --> 00:01:18.670
Island mobster, or if Soldier Boy worked for
him as a member of the La Cosa Nostra gang.

00:01:18.670 --> 00:01:22.610
But the file definitely shows us that Soldier
Boy was in jail when Vought discovered him.

00:01:22.610 --> 00:01:26.390
Despite his unsavory past, Vought still decided
to give him powers. It turns out Vought's

00:01:26.390 --> 00:01:31.090
penchant for creating ill-behaved superhumans
goes back to the very beginning of its history.

00:01:31.090 --> 00:01:35.680
To outsiders, Billy Joel's music probably
seems like a bizarre pairing with the extra

00:01:35.680 --> 00:01:39.990
raunchy, hyper-violent content of "The Boys."
But the Piano Man is intimately connected

00:01:39.990 --> 00:01:43.579
with the show. In the very first episode of
"The Boys," Hughie and his girlfriend Robin

00:01:43.579 --> 00:01:47.979
are having an intense debate about the merits
of Billy Joel's music when A-Train kills her.

00:01:47.979 --> 00:01:51.310
That incident might have changed Hughie's
relationship with his favorite recording artist,

00:01:51.310 --> 00:01:55.790
but it also marks the beginning of some great
needle drops in the Amazon Prime series.

00:01:55.790 --> 00:01:59.600
Season 2 features multiple Billy Joel songs,
and the artist was excited enough to tweet

00:01:59.600 --> 00:02:03.910
his support for the show. The connection continues
into Season 3, but this time the music is

00:02:03.910 --> 00:02:08.141
even more thematically appropriate. "Uptown
Girl" plays in the first episode of the season

00:02:08.141 --> 00:02:12.190
over a montage of Hughie's near-perfect life
with Annie and working for the Federal Bureau

00:02:12.190 --> 00:02:16.440
of Superhuman Affairs. The song marks a new
high point for Hughie, but it also comes back

00:02:16.440 --> 00:02:20.300
over the episode's credits just after Hughie
discovers that his boss is the head-popping

00:02:20.300 --> 00:02:24.610
supe who first begins terrorizing people back
in Season 2. Hughie might love some Billy

00:02:24.610 --> 00:02:27.770
Joel, but the music isn't always a good omen
for his immediate future.

00:02:27.770 --> 00:02:32.660
Most of the time, Billy Butcher comes across
as cold, calculating, and heartless. But every

00:02:32.660 --> 00:02:37.100
so often, there's a small hint indicating
that Butcher has a softer side. In "The Boys"

00:02:37.100 --> 00:02:40.510
comics, Butcher's dog Terror is a regular
member of the team. Butcher's affection for

00:02:40.510 --> 00:02:44.420
the bulldog shows that he does have the capacity
to care for something other than himself.

00:02:44.420 --> 00:02:49.260
He simply chooses to do so in an extremely
selective, Terror-specific capacity.

00:02:49.260 --> 00:02:53.560
Butcher's fur baby has a small role in Season
2, but for the most part, TV audiences have

00:02:53.560 --> 00:02:57.620
missed out on that part of Butcher's life.
That's probably because, as showrunner, Eric

00:02:57.620 --> 00:03:01.790
Kripke, knows how difficult it is to work
with animals. Ultimately, having Terror join

00:03:01.790 --> 00:03:05.310
The Boys on their misadventures regularly
turned out to be too much of a hassle.

00:03:05.310 --> 00:03:09.150
While Terror might not be helping in the effort
to bring down Homelander in Season 3, he isn't

00:03:09.150 --> 00:03:13.060
absent from the show entirely. This season
sees Butcher spruce up his kitchen counter

00:03:13.060 --> 00:03:17.181
at home with a cookie jar that looks identical
to Terror. Beneath that tough exterior, Butcher

00:03:17.181 --> 00:03:19.870
is a softy when it comes to dogs.

00:03:19.870 --> 00:03:23.690
TV shows cost millions of dollars to put together
and raising that kind of money can be a real

00:03:23.690 --> 00:03:27.760
challenge. Most shows, and even movies, tend
to include some product placement to pay the

00:03:27.760 --> 00:03:31.540
bills. Even though Amazon certainly isn't
hurting for money, "The Boys" has done the

00:03:31.540 --> 00:03:35.780
same. Seeing real-world products in a fictional
universe can sometimes feel jarring, but "The

00:03:35.780 --> 00:03:39.820
Boys" has been able to include products in
a way that fits into the story in a more-or-less

00:03:39.820 --> 00:03:40.820
natural way.

00:03:40.820 --> 00:03:44.930
In Season 3, Hughie and Annie's relationship
is going better than ever, but it's not completely

00:03:44.930 --> 00:03:49.220
smooth sailing. Shortly after Homelander reveals
that he killed Supersonic, Hughie has his

00:03:49.220 --> 00:03:53.400
own bombshell to drop on Annie, so he decides
to soften the blow with some candy and White

00:03:53.400 --> 00:03:54.400
Claw.

00:03:54.400 --> 00:03:58.300
Whether or not the brand name boozy seltzer
seems out of place is up for debate, but the

00:03:58.300 --> 00:04:02.810
candy, Almond Joy, Charleston Chew, and Bit-O-Honey,
actually has real significance to the in-show

00:04:02.810 --> 00:04:07.250
history of "The Boys." During Season 2 when
Hughie and Annie start building their relationship,

00:04:07.250 --> 00:04:11.380
he finds out that those are her favorite kinds
of candy. At the time, Hughie calls out Annie's

00:04:11.380 --> 00:04:12.380
taste.

00:04:12.380 --> 00:04:15.890
"Those are like the three worst candy bars
in the history of candy."

00:04:15.890 --> 00:04:18.860
Clearly, he filed away the information for
later use.

00:04:18.860 --> 00:04:23.640
There have always been clear parallels between
Vought in the world of "The Boys" and Disney.

00:04:23.640 --> 00:04:27.310
Both companies have histories that go back
to the early 20th century, and both function

00:04:27.310 --> 00:04:30.910
as massive entertainment companies that also
pursue numerous side projects in the form

00:04:30.910 --> 00:04:35.250
of corporate subsidiaries. In Season 3, the
Vought-Disney associations become even more

00:04:35.250 --> 00:04:39.130
heavy-handed, and not just because we see
much more of Payback, the series' original

00:04:39.130 --> 00:04:41.730
in-universe answer to the Avengers, than usual.

00:04:41.730 --> 00:04:45.820
VoughtLand is exactly what you would expect
from an alternate reality Disneyland. Kimiko

00:04:45.820 --> 00:04:48.970
and Frenchie have to make their way through
a section of the park called Brave Maeve's

00:04:48.970 --> 00:04:53.541
Inclusive Kingdom, and their walk is packed
with hilarious details. Clearly, Vought is

00:04:53.541 --> 00:04:58.711
capitalizing on Maeve's identity in the way
only a soulless mega-conglomerate can. They

00:04:58.711 --> 00:05:02.610
also scramble to make the public forget about
the literal Nazi who occupied a prominent

00:05:02.610 --> 00:05:05.810
and influential position in The Seven until
very recently.

00:05:05.810 --> 00:05:10.160
On top of ridiculous caricatures of Vought
heroes and an unbelievable amount of rainbow

00:05:10.160 --> 00:05:14.230
flags, the Inclusive Kingdom also has some
searing jabs at corporate appropriation of

00:05:14.230 --> 00:05:18.070
social justice issues. Kimiko and Frenchie
walk past food vendors like "BLM BLTs," "Woke

00:05:18.070 --> 00:05:24.260
Wok," and a Mexican food stand with a menu
that includes "Systemically Oppressed Tacos."

00:05:24.260 --> 00:05:28.170
Vought isn't going to lose out on marketing
opportunities, no matter how shameless they

00:05:28.170 --> 00:05:29.170
are.

00:05:29.170 --> 00:05:32.410
"Truly there is no god here."

00:05:32.410 --> 00:05:36.700
The relationship between Homelander and Vought
executive Madelyn Stillwell was a major focus

00:05:36.700 --> 00:05:41.360
of the first season of "The Boys," which premiered
in 2019. Homelander's jealousy of Madelyn's

00:05:41.360 --> 00:05:45.230
son became a huge point of conflict for the
two of them, right up until Homelander burned

00:05:45.230 --> 00:05:48.980
a hole in Madelyn's head in the season finale.
In the years since that dramatic moment, the

00:05:48.980 --> 00:05:53.380
ultimate fate of Madelyn's son Teddy has been
a mystery, but Season 3 quietly reveals that

00:05:53.380 --> 00:05:54.990
Teddy is alive and well.

00:05:54.990 --> 00:05:59.260
Hughie's investigation of his boss Victoria
Neuman leads him to the Red River Institute.

00:05:59.260 --> 00:06:03.050
The Institute operates as a home for young
supes whose parents either abandoned them

00:06:03.050 --> 00:06:07.750
or died in a powers-related accident. When
Hughie arrives at Red River, he pretends that

00:06:07.750 --> 00:06:11.060
he and Annie are interested in adopting a
child so he can get a closer look at what's

00:06:11.060 --> 00:06:12.639
happening behind the scenes.

00:06:12.639 --> 00:06:18.720
"Well, Samir is South Asian. It's very hot
right now."

00:06:18.720 --> 00:06:22.620
In one of the Institute's classrooms, Hughie
has a brief interaction with a young teleporting

00:06:22.620 --> 00:06:27.270
boy who turns out to be little Teddy. The
season doesn't reveal how Teddy became a supe,

00:06:27.270 --> 00:06:30.940
but the reveal has some believing that Teddy
is actually Homelander's son. We'll have to

00:06:30.940 --> 00:06:33.910
wait until Season 4 for more details.

00:06:33.910 --> 00:06:37.720
Soldier Boy's history with Vought goes all
the way back to the company's earliest days.

00:06:37.720 --> 00:06:40.810
He's been out of the picture since the '80s,
but he has intimate knowledge of the first

00:06:40.810 --> 00:06:45.410
several decades of Vought's roster of supes,
and also made an outsized impact on supe culture.

00:06:45.410 --> 00:06:49.770
As Soldier Boy reveals, he's the founder of
the annual Herogasm event. When he discovers

00:06:49.770 --> 00:06:53.800
that Herogasm is still running almost four
decades after his apparent death, he clearly

00:06:53.800 --> 00:06:56.160
takes a little bit of pride in its longevity.

00:06:56.160 --> 00:07:00.370
Soldier Boy brags about his history with Herogasm
to Hughie and Butcher, and in doing so, he

00:07:00.370 --> 00:07:04.419
also reveals a shocking detail about his history
with Stormfront. He tells the others that

00:07:04.419 --> 00:07:09.020
he created the first Herogasm alongside her,
but calls her by a telling name.

00:07:09.020 --> 00:07:16.080
"I founded it back in '52. Me and this other
supe, Liberty. Man, was she a firecracker."

00:07:16.080 --> 00:07:20.090
Season 2 of "The Boys" revealed that Stormfront
was once called Liberty before Vought forced

00:07:20.090 --> 00:07:23.950
her to undergo a rebranding. Neither Hughie
nor Butcher tells Soldier Boy what became

00:07:23.950 --> 00:07:27.990
of his old friend, and as far as we know,
he doesn't discover Liberty's fate for himself

00:07:27.990 --> 00:07:32.320
before the end of Season 3. Considering Homelander's
intimate history with Stormfront, maybe it's

00:07:32.320 --> 00:07:35.720
better for everyone if this truth remains
buried.

00:07:35.720 --> 00:07:39.620
Before working on "The Boys," showrunner Eric
Kripke was the mastermind behind another wildly

00:07:39.620 --> 00:07:44.830
successful TV series. "Supernatural" ran for
well over 300 episodes on The CW, launched

00:07:44.830 --> 00:07:48.900
the career of Jensen Ackles, and inspired
a prequel series called "The Winchesters."

00:07:48.900 --> 00:07:53.169
Season 3 of "The Boys" sees Ackles join the
show as Soldier Boy, but he isn't the only

00:07:53.169 --> 00:07:55.700
"Supernatural" alumnus who's part of the Amazon
Prime series.

00:07:55.700 --> 00:07:59.620
In addition to Ackles, this season of "The
Boys" also includes appearances from other

00:07:59.620 --> 00:08:03.889
"Supernatural" actors. Gattlin Griffith plays
Gunpowder in flashback sequences this season,

00:08:03.889 --> 00:08:07.720
and he previously appeared as Jesse Turner
in the "Supernatural" episode "I Believe the

00:08:07.720 --> 00:08:09.090
Children Are Our Future."

00:08:09.090 --> 00:08:13.419
The season also reintroduces Jim Beaver as
presidential candidate Robert Singer. Beaver

00:08:13.419 --> 00:08:18.040
played the character Bobby Singer on "Supernatural,"
and both characters grew up in South Dakota.

00:08:18.040 --> 00:08:22.491
Dakota Bob, as he's called by some in "The
Boys," could become even more important in

00:08:22.491 --> 00:08:23.491
Season 4.

00:08:23.491 --> 00:08:27.520
"The Boys" is loaded with social and political
satire, but the show's main characters are

00:08:27.520 --> 00:08:31.250
so caught up in their battle against Vought
and the supes that they hardly ever have time

00:08:31.250 --> 00:08:35.180
to stop and directly address issues that also
exist in our world. A-Train and Blue Hawk

00:08:35.180 --> 00:08:39.180
provide an exception to this rule in Season
3 by offering their perspectives on police

00:08:39.180 --> 00:08:43.840
brutality and systemic racism. However, dipping
into the discourse of current events doesn't

00:08:43.840 --> 00:08:45.750
pan out so hot for either of them.

00:08:45.750 --> 00:08:49.690
Despite the prominence of supes in this universe,
there's plenty of overlap between the political

00:08:49.690 --> 00:08:54.520
history of "The Boys" and our own. Some background
details in Season 3 can help fans better understand

00:08:54.520 --> 00:08:58.680
how one character, in particular, relates
to some of the most important issues in recent

00:08:58.680 --> 00:08:59.680
history.

00:08:59.680 --> 00:09:02.980
M.M.'s apartment becomes a regular hangout
for the characters this season, and in addition

00:09:02.980 --> 00:09:07.860
to meticulously cleaning the place, M.M. has
also decorated it with political memorabilia.

00:09:07.860 --> 00:09:12.350
Hanging above his TV is a "Move on over, or
we'll move on over you" sign that was adopted

00:09:12.350 --> 00:09:17.250
by the Black Panthers in the 1960s. In shots
of M.M.'s entryway, fans can spot a framed

00:09:17.250 --> 00:09:21.390
picture of President Barack Obama hung above
a "Vote Huey Newton" poster.

00:09:21.390 --> 00:09:25.260
M.M. himself is much too young to have been
a member of the Black Panthers, but it's not

00:09:25.260 --> 00:09:28.950
a stretch to imagine that his father had time
to participate in the movement in between

00:09:28.950 --> 00:09:31.840
his legal battles with Vought and Soldier
Boy.

00:09:31.840 --> 00:09:36.110
When it comes to attention to detail, "The
Boys" goes above and beyond most other shows.

00:09:36.110 --> 00:09:39.860
It's not just that "The Boys" is filled with
references to other series or small details

00:09:39.860 --> 00:09:44.570
about the history of its world. The team behind
Amazon's series also takes care to accurately

00:09:44.570 --> 00:09:48.980
recreate the feel of ridiculous commercials
and cable news segments. They also never miss

00:09:48.980 --> 00:09:53.180
an opportunity to sneak in a joke, which means
that any time a news segment is on screen,

00:09:53.180 --> 00:09:56.720
the ticker at the bottom of the screen is
running with some hilarious detail.

00:09:56.720 --> 00:10:01.080
In Season 3, the trend of ticker jokes begins
in the very first episode. The news is covering

00:10:01.080 --> 00:10:05.040
a group of Stormfront sympathizers who call
themselves Stormchasers. The ticker at the

00:10:05.040 --> 00:10:09.601
bottom explains that some Stormchasers have
gathered to await the return of JFK Jr. It's

00:10:09.601 --> 00:10:13.750
something some real-life conspiracy theorists
believed would happen back in 2021.

00:10:13.750 --> 00:10:20.270
"They were disappointed when the assassinated
president and his deceased son never showed."

00:10:20.270 --> 00:10:23.840
The news ticker jokes in "The Boys" are almost
always poking fun at some real-life figure

00:10:23.840 --> 00:10:28.971
or group. In the Season 3 finale, a news ticker
takes a jab at Texas Senator Ted Cruz. It

00:10:28.971 --> 00:10:32.370
says that Cruz was caught trying to leave
a hotel without paying for the pornography

00:10:32.370 --> 00:10:36.790
that he ordered in his room. It's those kinds
of small details that make "The Boys" spectacular

00:10:36.790 --> 00:10:38.680
to watch again and again.

00:10:38.680 --> 00:10:43.330
